{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,23]],"date-time":"2026-10-23T20:24:38Z","timestamp":1792787078106,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":188,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"1","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2015,12,23]],"date-time":"2015-12-23T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1450828800000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"funder":[{"name":"Health Education England (HEE)"},{"name":"Office for Fair Access (OFFA)"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["asmepublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Medical Education"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Context<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Selection methods used by medical schools should reliably identify whether candidates are likely to be successful in medical training and ultimately become competent clinicians. However, there is little consensus regarding methods that reliably evaluate non\u2010academic attributes, and longitudinal studies examining predictors of success after qualification are insufficient. This systematic review synthesises the extant research evidence on the relative strengths of various selection methods. We offer a research agenda and identify key considerations to inform policy and practice in the next 50\u00a0years.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>A formalised literature search was conducted for studies published between 1997 and 2015. A total of 194 articles met the inclusion criteria and were appraised in relation to: (i) selection method used; (ii) research question(s) addressed, and (iii) type of study design.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>\n                      Eight selection methods were identified: (i) aptitude tests; (ii) academic records; (iii) personal statements; (iv) references; (v) situational judgement tests (\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">SJT<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s); (vi) personality and emotional intelligence assessments; (vii) interviews and multiple mini\u2010interviews (\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">MMI<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s), and (viii) selection centres (\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">SC<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s). The evidence relating to each method was reviewed against four evaluation criteria: effectiveness (reliability and validity); procedural issues; acceptability, and cost\u2010effectiveness.\n                    <\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusions<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>\n                      Evidence shows clearly that academic records,\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">MMI<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s, aptitude tests,\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">SJT<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s and\n                      <jats:styled-content style=\"fixed-case\">SC<\/jats:styled-content>\n                      s are more effective selection methods and are generally fairer than traditional interviews, references and personal statements. However, achievement in different selection methods may differentially predict performance at the various stages of medical education and clinical practice. Research into selection has been over\u2010reliant on cross\u2010sectional study designs and has tended to focus on reliability estimates rather than validity as an indicator of quality. A comprehensive framework of outcome criteria should be developed to allow researchers to interpret empirical evidence and compare selection methods fairly. This review highlights gaps in evidence for the combination of selection tools that is most effective and the weighting to be given to each tool.\n                    <\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","DOI":"10.1111\/medu.12817","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2015,12,23]],"date-time":"2015-12-23T06:51:33Z","timestamp":1450853493000},"page":"36-60","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":402,"title":["How effective are selection methods in medical education? A systematic review"],"prefix":"10.1111","volume":"50","author":[{"given":"Fiona","family":"Patterson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Organisational Psychology City University London UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alec","family":"Knight","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Work Psychology Group Derby UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jon","family":"Dowell","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Medicine University of Dundee  Dundee UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sandra","family":"Nicholson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Barts and The London School of Medicine and Dentistry Queen Mary University of London London UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fran","family":"Cousans","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Work Psychology Group Derby UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jennifer","family":"Cleland","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Medicine and Dentistry University of Aberdeen Aberdeen UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","pu